  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"109\" data-end=\"304\"><strong data-start=\"109\" data-end=\"124\">Haptoglobin</strong> is a plasma glycoprotein produced by the liver that binds free hemoglobin released from red blood cells. This prevents hemoglobin-induced oxidative damage and helps conserve iron.</p>\n<p data-start=\"306\" data-end=\"335\">🩺 <strong data-start=\"309\" data-end=\"335\">Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"531\">\n<li data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"376\">\n<p data-start=\"338\" data-end=\"376\">Used to evaluate <strong data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"375\">hemolytic anemia</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"377\" data-end=\"431\">\n<p data-start=\"379\" data-end=\"431\">Helps distinguish between different types of anemia.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"432\" data-end=\"531\">\n<p data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"531\">Also an <strong data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"466\">acute-phase reactant</strong>, so levels may increase with inflammation, infection, or trauma.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Elevated Haptoglobin</strong> May Indicate:</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inflammation or infection</strong> &ndash; As an acute-phase reactant, levels rise in response to systemic inflammation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tissue damage or trauma</strong> &ndash; Elevated due to stress response or injury.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Certain malignancies or autoimmune conditions</strong> &ndash; May show elevated levels in chronic disease settings.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
